","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-7ae36d8b6bc5","stem":"Mention 5 investigations which can be performed at hospital level for a patient who is in coma.","type":"Clinical Vignette","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-9e2fa099e3d8","stem":"A 47 years old woman with diabetic mellitus for 10 years came to the outpatient clinic for follow up. Her FBG was 16mmol/l and BP 120/70mmHg. Urinalysis revealed protein ++ with no white blood cells in urine. The first thing to control her proteinuria from progressing to chronic renal failure is to","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Start ACE inhibitor"},{"label":"B","text":"Decrease protein diet"},{"label":"C","text":"Control her blood pressure"},{"label":"D","text":"Strictly control of glycaemia"},{"label":"E","text":"Counsel on carbohydrate diet"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-af2d708c450b","stem":"A 21 years old female presents at outpatient clinic with four days history of high grade fever, lower abdominal pain and dysuria. On examination, temperature was 390C, abdominal examination reveals severe loin angles tenderness. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Acute urinary tract obstruction"},{"label":"B","text":"Acute glomerulonephritis"},{"label":"C","text":"Acute pyelonephritis"},{"label":"D","text":"Severe renal stones"},{"label":"E","text":"Cystitis"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-094970a488e9","stem":"The diagnosis of rheumatic fever in a patient with an elevated antistreptolysin O titer is confirmed by","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Fever with an elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate"},{"label":"B","text":"Carditis and elevated erythrocyte sedimentation rate"},{"label":"C","text":"Arthralgia and previous history of rheumatic fever"},{"label":"D","text":"Prolonged P-R interval on ECG and fever"},{"label":"E","text":"Erythema nodosum and arthritis"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":[]},{"id":"q-8ba4e4884115","stem":"A 34 years old female presents with a history of on and off palpitation, headache and sweating for one month. Her blood pressure at home was found to be 190/120mmHg. Three hours later when she arrived at the hospital, her blood pressure was 130/90 mmHg, no treatment was given at home. The most likely diagnosis is","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"ENT","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Primary aldosteronism 1"},{"label":"B","text":"Primary hypertension"},{"label":"C","text":"Phaechromocytoma"},{"label":"D","text":"Anxiety disorder"},{"label":"E","text":"Renin tumor"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"ENT","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"not_adjudicated","sourceAnswerTrusted":false,"publicationStatus":"unverified_draft","auditFlags":["repeated_question_stem","topic_generic"],"cleaningOperations":["option_whitespace_normalization"],"duplicateStemGroup":"0d4c8b807ceb9b49229938e95cd7b6550c55da8f"},{"id":"q-fee3367c81ca","stem":"A middle aged man present with acute onset of high grade fever and pleuritic chest pain. He also complains of productive cough rusty in colour. Physical examination reveals dyspnea, febrile 390C and features of consolidation on left infrascapular. What is the most likely diagnosis?","type":"Single Best Answer MCQ","subject":"Internal Medicine","topic":"Unassigned topic","topicOriginal":"General","subtopic":"","suggestedTopic":null,"options":[{"label":"A","text":"Lung abscess"},{"label":"B","text":"Bronchial asthma"},{"label":"C","text":"Lobar pneumonia"},{"label":"D","text":"Bronchopneumonia"},{"label":"E","text":"Pulmonary Tuberculosis"}],"priority":1,"status":"practice_draft","flags":["topic_generic"],"keyVerified":false,"hasSourceAnswer":false,"needsFigure":false,"displaySubject":"Internal Medicine","displayTopic":"Unassigned topic","answerAuditStatus":"r